course: dictionary
lesson: 3124
title: Electromagnets
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A magnet pulls on iron and steel. An electromagnet is a magnet you can switch on and off, because it uses electricity. «Electromagnets are made with electricity and can be switched on and off.» Wrap wire into a coil, put an iron core inside, and pass electricity through the wire.
explain: Split the word into two parts: electro means electricity, and magnet is the pulling force you already know. So an electromagnet is a magnet powered by electricity. It only works while the electricity is on. When you switch the electricity off, it stops pulling.
explain: In class you can make one with a battery, some wire and an iron nail. Test it by counting how many paper clips it picks up. Then switch it off and see what happens to the clips.
fact: An electromagnet is a magnet made by passing electricity through a coil of wire.
fact: The coil is often wrapped around an iron core.
fact: An electromagnet works only while the electricity is on.
fact: Electromagnets are used in scrapyard cranes, doorbells, speakers, motors and MRI scanners.
fact: Useful phrases: make an electromagnet, switch off the electromagnet, coil of wire.
exercise: What makes an electromagnet a magnet? | It is made of wood | It is painted red | It has no wire at all | *It has electricity passing through a coil of wire
exercise: «Electromagnets are made with electricity and can be switched on and off.» When does an electromagnet stop pulling? | Never | Only when it is very cold | *When the electricity is switched off | When it is put in water
exercise: Which of these uses an electromagnet? | A pencil case | A paper cup | *A scrapyard crane | A wooden spoon
test: Why is a coil of wire needed? | *To carry electricity round the core so it becomes a magnet | To make it look nice | To hold the paper clips in place | To make the magnet heavier
test: How can you show that your electromagnet is working? | Shake it hard | Paint the nail | Put it in a drawer | *Count the paper clips it picks up
exam: A pupil says an electromagnet stays magnetic after the battery is removed. What is the correct answer? | Yes, it stays magnetic forever | Yes, because iron is always magnetic | Only in summer | *No, it works only while the electricity is on
